Q3 58%. Day staffing at both homes did not change during either quarter. Source note: Q2 times come from paper forms. The time field was blank on 3 forms, and the administrator assigned those from the shift recorded. Q3 times are stamped automatically by Tallyline when the entry is made, which may be some time after the fall was discovered.\n- Replies from the two registered managers to the Director of Operations, 7 October 2026 (manager-account): Penmor Lodge manager: 'I don't think it's the rota. Since Hollin Street Medical Practice took over our residents in May, the new GPs have been reviewing everyone's medication, and some residents have been started on night-time tablets. I'd look there first.' Ashby Fold manager: 'We moved to Hollin Street in May as well. My worry is admissions. We've taken more people from the hospital discharge-to-assess beds this summer, and they arrive frailer than our usual referrals. The rota is tight, but I'm not convinced it's the whole story.'\n\nBRIEF:\nThe evidence doesn't show that the three-person night rota caused the rise in falls, and it doesn't rule it out. Q3 falls (3.7 per 1,000 bed days at both homes) are inside each home's historical range. Q2 was unusually low, near the bottom of each home's range. The app also went live on 1 July, and the Q2 handover-note check found three clear unreported falls and three ambiguous entries in the six weeks that could be checked. That points to Q2 being under-recorded and the app capturing more, especially no-harm and \"found on floor\" falls. Harm-level falls rose only slightly. Frailer admissions and new GP prescribing are untested. Nobody can say whether call bells are slower, because no pre-June timings exist.\n\nI'd keep the pilot under its existing pause criteria. Re-present falls as rates, with the recording change marked. Start manual sampling of night call-bell response times now. Review the rollout in late January with Q4 data. Reversing the rota now is premature. Warnings to 